{
  "status": "PROPOSAL",
  "note": "Ship economics and drive rules (2026-11-03, second pass). Physical facts of the Cobrett-I N1 and OCS-4 come from the runtime (EliteCobraMk1.cpp, src/game/catalog/ships/ShipCargoPassports.h); every other class, every money / fuel / wear number is a PROPOSAL anchored on the constants in 'anchors'. Currency: Cr (one currency).",
  "anchors": {
    "pilot_wage_cr_per_month": 4000,
    "reactor_fuel_cr_per_kg": 600,
    "jump_vs_insystem_cost_ratio": 2.5,
    "note": "Reactor fuel = D/He-3 pellets (fusion, ~3.5e14 J/kg, 30 % plant-to-drive). Jump charge = the drive's consumable isotope charge (refined at fuel depots). The jump-charge price is DERIVED in logistics.py so that one network-mean jump of a half-loaded Cobrett costs jump_vs_insystem_cost_ratio x one mean in-system leg (author wish 2-3x)."
  },
  "cargo_model": {
    "container": "C20",
    "container_payload_t": 20.0,
    "container_volume_m3": 23.3,
    "container_tare_t": 1.7,
    "typical_mixed_density_t_m3": 0.4,
    "note": "Authoritative capacity model. '20-ton container' = class C20: up to 20 t PAYLOAD in 23.3 m3 (tare 1.7 t). A hull has a whole number of C20 places; its structural / thrust payload limit can bind before the places are full (OCS-4). Real loads are volume-limited for ordinary goods: mixed cargo ~0.4 t/m3 -> ~9 t per C20, so a 'full' Cobrett with mixed goods carries ~56 t; dense cargo (metals, ore samples, munitions) reaches the 120 t limit. The old 50 t passport value was a typical mixed load, the old 40 t traffic value an average ~70 % load; both are now derived."
  },
  "jump_rule": {
    "form": "fuel_t = q * d_ly * (alpha_t + M_total_t); alpha_t = kappa_t_per_m2 * hull_envelope_m2",
    "target_mean_jumps_empty": 3.0,
    "target_mean_jumps_full": 2.5,
    "reference_class": "cobrett_i_n1",
    "charge_hours_per_jump": 6.0,
    "charge_power_share_of_reactor": 0.25,
    "discharge_seconds": 2.0,
    "note": "Two physical terms instead of a pure mass law: the drive has to build its field over the whole hull envelope (alpha, proportional to envelope area) and to carry the mass inside it (M). Cargo inside the hull adds mass but not area, so a loaded small ship loses range moderately, and a long train of modules pays mostly the mass term (cheap per tonne). q and kappa are calibrated on the reference class so that a full Cobrett tank gives target_mean_jumps_empty / _full network-mean jumps. CONSUMABLE (jump charge, t), ENERGY (J, from the reactor during charging) and PEAK POWER (from the core's superconducting store during the discharge) are separate quantities: energy = charge power x charge time; peak = energy / discharge time. Peak power is never derived from the charge mass."
  },
  "insystem_rule": {
    "mean_leg_au": 1.2,
    "burn_accel_g": 1.0,
    "plant_efficiency": 0.3,
    "fuel_energy_j_per_kg": 350000000000000.0,
    "waste_heat_share": 0.7,
    "note": "A leg = station <-> jump-safe point or between two in-system ports: brachistochrone at the class burn_accel_g (loaded); drive power scales with the thrust fraction used (electrical input x thrust needed / max thrust). Thermal load = waste_heat_share of the drive input, rejected by the radiators."
  },
